AB  - Abstract Southern corn rust (SCR), caused by Puccinia polysora , is a destructive foliar disease that threatens maize production worldwide. Excessive dependence on synthetic chemical fungicides has driven urgent demand for eco-friendly biocontrol alternatives based on beneficial microorganisms. In this study, two endophytic fungal isolates, Purpureocillium lilacinum PL6 and Beauveria bassiana YC-1, were recovered from maize leaves and evaluated as dual-purpose biocontrol candidates in maize using morphological observation, molecular phylogenetic identification, whole-genome sequencing, indoor bioassays, and insect pathogenicity tests. Illumina-based genome assembly yielded 36.68 Mb and 33.05 Mb draft for strains PL6 and YC-1, respectively. Functional annotation against the COG and KEGG databases revealed that both genomes are enriched in functional genes linked to carbohydrate, amino acid and energy metabolism, consistent with the typical metabolic plasticity of entomopathogenic fungi. In planta inoculation assays verified that root drench application of PL6 and YC-1 significantly suppressed SCR progression: at 24 days post pathogen inoculation, disease incidence dropped to below 4% for PL6 treatment and approximately 21% for YC-1 treatment, respectively, whereas the untreated control reached nearly 48%. Meanwhile, both strains exhibited pathogenicity against fifth-instar larvae of Spodoptera frugip
