AB  - Spodoptera frugiperda, the fall armyworm (FAW), is a major global pest of different crops and especially maize, posing significant challenges due to its rapid spread and adaptive biology. The current investigation evaluated the effect of three insecticidal agents-Beauveria bassiana (Bb), Bacillus thuringiensis (Bt), and emamectin benzoate (eb)-applied alone and in respective dual combinations (Bb + Bt, Bb + eb, Bt + eb) against 3rd and 5th instar larvae of FAW. Results showed that the Bb + eb combination consistently induced the highest mortality (92.6 %), disrupted developmental parameters, reduced egg eclosion (15.0 %), and adult emergence (19.4 %), compared to the other treatments and controls. Sublethal doses of this combination prolonged larval and pupal durations (21.2 and 12.2 days, respectively) and significantly decreased adult longevity (9.4 days) compared to the other treatments and controls. These results suggest the potential of integrating chemical and microbial agents to improve pest control while moderating the chemical input. These findings support the strategic use of combined treatments targeting the development of FAW and offering a promising potential strategy for sustainable pest management.
