AB  - Abstract Fall armyworm (FAW), S. frugiperda is one of the destructive invasive pests of sweet corn, which causes a significant economic losses worldwide. Entomopathogenic fungi (EPF) is an alternative option to control this pest but have very specific efficacy depending upon the strains used. Thus, this study was designed to compare the efficacy of three indigenous strains of EPFs including Metarhizium anisopliae , Beauveria bassiana , and Isaria fumosorosea against third-instar FAW larvae. Sweet corn leaf disks were immersed in conidial formulation ranging from 104 to 108 conidia mL1, and then provided to the larvae in the Peter dish and larval mortality was recorded over 7 days. The results demonstrated that M. anisopliae was the most potent isolate, with LC50 of 1.42 105 conidia mL1, making it 4.7-fold more potent than B. bassiana and 12.3-fold more potent than I. fumosorosea . At a dosage of 1 107 conidia mL1, M. anisopliae killed 50% of larvae in 3.8 days (LT50), significantly faster than B. bassiana (5.2 days) and I. fumosorosea (7.1 days). M. anisopliae induced the highest mycosis rate (94.2%), the greatest feeding cessation index 80% by day 3, the strongest growth inhibition (82.7%), and the most severe disruption of pupation (73.9%). All isolates met quali
